<p>The <strong>Kindle Scribe Colorsoft</strong> at $80 off is the best absolute discount, but the <strong>Paperwhite Signature Edition</strong> at $159.99 is the best value for most EPUB readers — it adds wireless charging, auto-adjusting warm light, and 32 GB of storage for just $35 more than the base Paperwhite.</p>

<h2 id="which-kindle-for-epub">Which Kindle Should EPUB Readers Buy?</h2>

<p>Every current Kindle model supports EPUB natively through Amazon's Send to Kindle service. You can email EPUB files to your Kindle address, use the Kindle app on your phone or computer to push EPUBs wirelessly, or transfer them via USB in Calibre. The experience is not identical to native EPUB rendering on a Kobo or PocketBook — Kindle converts EPUB to its internal KFX format on the server side — but for most books the result is excellent.</p>

<p>Here is how each model stacks up for EPUB readers specifically:</p>

<h3>Kindle Paperwhite Signature Edition — Best All-Rounder</h3>

<p>The Paperwhite Signature Edition hits the sweet spot for EPUB readers. At $159.99 during the sale, you get:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>6.8-inch 300 PPI display</strong> — crisp enough for complex EPUB layouts, footnotes, and code blocks in technical ebooks</li>
  <li><strong>Auto-adjusting warm light</strong> — shifts from cool to warm as the day progresses, reducing eye strain during long reading sessions</li>
  <li><strong>32 GB storage</strong> — holds roughly 20,000 EPUB books, or 150 EPUBs with heavy image content</li>
  <li><strong>Wireless charging</strong> — pairs with any Qi charger, convenient for bedside docking</li>
  <li><strong>IPX8 waterproof rating</strong> — read EPUBs by the pool or in the bath</li>
</ul>

<p>If you read EPUB books from multiple sources — Project Gutenberg, Standard Ebooks, Kobo purchases you converted to EPUB, DRM-free Humble Bundle books — the Signature Edition's storage and wireless convenience make it the best choice.</p>

<h3>Kindle Scribe Colorsoft — Best for Note-Taking + EPUB</h3>

<p>The Kindle Scribe Colorsoft at $369.99 ($80 off) is overkill for pure EPUB reading — the 10.2-inch E Ink Kaleido 3 display is heavier and more expensive than any standard reader. But it is the <strong>only Kindle that lets you annotate EPUB files with a stylus</strong> and see the annotations in color. If you read technical EPUBs, academic papers, or cookbooks where color matters (charts, diagrams, photographs), the Colorsoft's color E Ink display and note-taking functionality make it a legitimate EPUB workstation.</p>

<p>Note that the Colorsoft's color resolution drops to 150 PPI in color mode (300 PPI in grayscale). For pure black-and-white EPUB reading, the standard Scribe at $339.99 is functionally identical and $30 cheaper.</p>

<h3>Base Kindle (11th Gen) — Best Budget Option</h3>

<p>The base Kindle at $79.99 is a capable EPUB reader. It has the same 300 PPI display as the Paperwhite (just smaller at 6 inches) and supports all Send to Kindle EPUB workflows. What you give up: no warm light, no waterproofing, 16 GB instead of 32 GB, and a lower-resolution front light that can look uneven in dark rooms. For casual EPUB reading on a tight budget, it is still a fantastic device at this price.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="buy-now-or-wait">Buy Now or Wait for the New Kindles?</h2>

<p>If you have been following <a href="/blog/kindle-2026-lineup-refresh-coming-soon/">our coverage of the rumored Kindle lineup refresh</a>, you know that Amazon is reportedly rebuilding its entire Kindle lineup to comply with the EU Battery Law, which takes effect in 2027. New models with replaceable batteries are expected in late 2026 — possibly as soon as October or November.</p>

<p>Here is how to decide whether to buy now or wait:</p>

<p><strong>Buy now if:</strong> you want a Kindle for EPUB reading today and the savings matter to you. The Paperwhite Signature Edition at $159.99 is a genuinely good price — 20 percent off the regular price. The new models will almost certainly launch at full price (likely $10-20 higher than current MSRPs due to the battery redesign), so you would pay more and wait 3-4 months. If you <a href="/blog/amazon-ending-support-13-kindles-2026/">own an older Kindle that Amazon just ended support for</a>, this sale is your best replacement price.</p>

<p><strong>Wait if:</strong> battery longevity is your primary concern. The EU Battery Law will require user-replaceable batteries in portable devices by 2027. Current Kindles have glued-in batteries that cost $50-80 to replace professionally. A 2027-compliant Kindle with a slide-out or clip-in battery would last a decade instead of 3-4 years. If your current e-reader still works, the eco-friendly argument for waiting is strong.</p>

<p><strong>Split the difference:</strong> buy the base Kindle at $79.99 now and upgrade to the new model when it launches. At $80, the base Kindle is cheap enough that you can hand it down to a family member when the 2027 models arrive. This is the most pragmatic path if you need a device immediately but want the long-term battery benefit.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="epub-workflow">How to Read EPUB Books on Your New Kindle</h2>

<p>Once you buy a Kindle, here is how to get EPUB books onto it:</p>

<h3>Method 1: Send to Kindle (Easiest)</h3>

<p>Go to <strong>Amazon's Send to Kindle page</strong> (or use the Kindle app on your phone). Drag and drop any EPUB file — it is converted on Amazon's servers and pushed to your Kindle wirelessly within 60 seconds. EPUBs sent this way sync your reading position, highlights, and notes across all your Kindle devices. The conversion preserves the EPUB's table of contents, images, and basic formatting.</p>

<h3>Method 2: Calibre + USB (Best for Large Libraries)</h3>

<p>If you have a Calibre library with hundreds of EPUBs, USB transfer is faster than Send to Kindle. <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Our full Calibre EPUB to Kindle workflow guide</a> covers the process in detail, but the short version is:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Connect your Kindle to a computer via USB</li>
  <li>Open Calibre and select the EPUB books you want to transfer</li>
  <li>Calibre auto-converts EPUB to AZW3 (Kindle's native format) and transfers directly to the device</li>
  <li>Eject the Kindle — books appear in your library immediately</li>
</ol>

<p>Calibre gives you control over the conversion output — you can adjust font sizes, line spacing, margins, and even embed custom fonts into the EPUB before it reaches your Kindle.</p>

<h3>Method 3: Email to Kindle (Wireless, No App Needed)</h3>

<p>Every Kindle has a unique @kindle.com email address (find it in Settings → Your Account → Send-to-Kindle Email). Attach an EPUB file to an email with "Convert" in the subject line, send it, and the EPUB appears on your Kindle within minutes. This method works from any email client and requires no software installation.</p>

