<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Are Best Buy Kindle deals the same as Amazon Prime Day prices?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>In most cases, yes. Best Buy matched Amazon's Prime Day pricing on Kindle devices, and in some configurations — like the Kindle Scribe Colorsoft — Best Buy is offering the same $80 discount that Amazon had. The sale runs through July 26, 2026. Best Buy also offers its own extended return policy (15 days standard, longer with a Best Buy Plus/Total membership) which can be more flexible than Amazon's.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Can I read EPUB books on Kindle without converting?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>EPUB files cannot be read directly on a Kindle without going through Amazon's Send to Kindle conversion pipeline. When you email a .epub file to your Kindle address or use the Send to Kindle website, Amazon converts the EPUB to KFX (Kindle's internal format) on the server side. The result looks nearly identical to the original EPUB, but the conversion is not lossless — some complex CSS layouts, embedded fonts, and SVG images may not survive intact. For most novels and standard non-fiction, the conversion is invisible. For image-heavy or precisely-formatted EPUBs, a Kobo or PocketBook is a better choice since both read EPUB natively.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Will Best Buy price-match if the Kindle goes on sale again?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Best Buy's standard price match policy does not apply to post-purchase price drops unless you have a Best Buy Plus or Total membership. With Plus ($49.99/year), you get 60 days of price match protection on products purchased at Best Buy. Without it, you have 15 days for most items, but price match is at Best Buy's discretion on clearance or limited-time deals. If you are concerned about a post-sale price drop, the safest approach is to buy with a credit card that offers price protection benefits.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Can I sideload EPUB books from my computer without Wi-Fi?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Yes. Connect your Kindle to a computer via USB, and it appears as an external drive. Copy EPUB files into the <code>documents</code> folder, then eject the Kindle. The Kindle will convert them automatically during the indexing process. Calibre automates this workflow and handles the conversion to AZW3 format on your computer before transfer, giving you more control over formatting than the on-device conversion. This method does not require Wi-Fi, Amazon account, or Send to Kindle.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Should I buy a Kindle or a Kobo for EPUB reading in 2026?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>Kindle and Kobo serve different EPUB reading needs. A Kindle is better if you buy most of your books from Amazon (which now offers DRM-free EPUB downloads) and want the seamless Send to Kindle ecosystem. A Kobo is better if you read EPUB files from multiple sources — library borrows through OverDrive/Libby, DRM-free stores like Standard Ebooks and Project Gutenberg, or self-published EPUBs — because Kobo reads EPUB natively without any server-side conversion. Kobo also integrates directly with OverDrive for library lending, which is a significant advantage for EPUB readers in the US, UK, Canada, and other supported regions. For a full comparison, see our <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook 2026 guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

